-- Q10846: Partnerships [medium/L3]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Dawson must contribute $15,000 in cash to restore the deficit balance","The partnership must allocate the first $15,000 of income to Dawson to eliminate the deficit","The deficit is forgiven and no further corrective action is required","The partnership must distribute $15,000 to other partners to offset the deficit"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Treas. Reg. Section 1.704-1(b)(2)(ii)(d), a qualified income offset requires the partnership to allocate items of income and gain to a partner with an unexpected capital account deficit as quickly as possible to eliminate that deficit. This preserves economic effect as an alternative to a deficit restoration obligation.
Wrong (A): A qualified income offset replaces the need for a cash restoration; Dawson is not required to contribute funds.
Wrong (C): The deficit is not forgiven; the qualified income offset mechanism specifically addresses it through future income allocations.
Wrong (D): Distributions to other partners do not correct a capital account deficit; only income allocations to the deficit partner can.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 10846;

-- Q10850: Partnerships [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["As a guaranteed payment subject to self-employment tax under Section 707(c)","As a distribution that reduces Quinn''s outside basis in the partnership","As a tax-free fringe benefit excluded from Quinn''s gross income","As a Section 707(a) payment, deductible by the partnership and ordinary income to Quinn"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 707(a)(1), when a partner transacts with the partnership in a capacity other than as a partner at arm''s-length rates, the transaction is treated as between the partnership and a third party. Quinn billed at her standard rate and the payment was not tied to partnership income, making this a Section 707(a) transaction.
Wrong (A): Guaranteed payments under Section 707(c) are determined without regard to partnership income, but Quinn acted as an independent service provider, not in her capacity as a partner.
Wrong (B): This is not a distribution because Quinn performed services in a non-partner capacity at arm''s length.
Wrong (C): Payments for services are not excludable fringe benefits; they are ordinary income to the recipient.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 10850;

-- Q10856: Partnerships [hard/L4]
