-- Q10907: Alternative Minimum Tax [hard/L4]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The AMT computation is unchanged because SALT is fully added back regardless of the cap","AMT increases because the SALT cap reduces regular tax liability more than it reduces AMTI","The SALT cap reduces the AMT add-back from $35,000 to $10,000, narrowing the AMT gap","The TCJA eliminated the AMT entirely for taxpayers residing in high-tax states"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 56(b)(1)(A), only the SALT deduction actually claimed on the regular return is added back for AMT. Pre-TCJA, Brennan would deduct and add back $35,000. Under TCJA, only $10,000 is deducted and added back, reducing the AMT adjustment by $25,000 and narrowing the gap between regular taxable income and AMTI.
Wrong (A): The AMT add-back equals the deduction claimed, not the amount of taxes paid, so the cap does change the computation.
Wrong (B): The SALT cap actually narrows the AMT differential rather than increasing AMT exposure.
Wrong (D): The TCJA did not eliminate the AMT; it increased the exemption amount and phaseout thresholds.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 10907;

-- Q10914: Alternative Minimum Tax [easy/L1]
