-- Q11145: S Corporations [hard/L4]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Archer''s S election automatically terminates because S corporations cannot carry E&P","Archer inherits the E&P under Section 381, creating Section 1375 excess passive income risk","The $200,000 E&P is eliminated upon acquisition because the acquirer is an S corporation","Archer inherits the E&P but it is immediately distributed tax-free to all shareholders"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 381(c)(2), the acquiring corporation in a tax-free reorganization inherits the target''s accumulated E&P. An S corporation can carry accumulated E&P from acquisitions, exposing it to the Section 1375 excess net passive income tax and the three-year termination rule under Section 1362(d)(3).
Wrong (A): Inheriting E&P does not itself terminate the S election; it merely creates exposure if passive income exceeds 25% of gross receipts for three consecutive years.
Wrong (C): Section 381 requires the acquirer to inherit E&P regardless of its tax status; the E&P is not eliminated.
Wrong (D): There is no automatic distribution of inherited E&P; it remains on Archer''s books as accumulated E&P.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11145;

-- Q11190: Business Law: Contracts [hard/L4]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Lost profits are automatically recoverable as direct damages in any breach of contract","Lost profits are never recoverable in contract law because they are inherently speculative","Lost profits are recoverable only if Summit knew or should have known of the resale","Lost profits are recoverable regardless of foreseeability if their exact amount is proven"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under the rule from Hadley v. Baxendale, codified in Restatement (Second) of Contracts Section 351, consequential damages such as lost profits are recoverable only if reasonably foreseeable to the breaching party at contract formation. Because the contract was silent about the downstream sale, Baxter must prove Summit knew or had reason to know of the resale.
Wrong (A): Lost profits are consequential, not direct damages; direct damages cover only the $45,000 in spoiled goods.
Wrong (B): Lost profits are not categorically barred; they are recoverable when the foreseeability requirement is met.
Wrong (D): Proving exact amount does not eliminate the foreseeability requirement established by Hadley v. Baxendale.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11190;

-- Q11194: Business Law: Contracts [medium/L3]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Only if the actual damages suffered exceed the liquidated damages amount","If the amount was a reasonable estimate of harm and actual damages were difficult to calculate","If the breaching party agreed to the clause in the contract, regardless of reasonableness","Only if the non-breaching party proves it suffered exactly $30,000 in actual harm"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Restatement (Second) of Contracts Section 356, a liquidated damages clause is enforceable when (1) the amount is a reasonable forecast of anticipated harm at the time of contracting, and (2) actual damages are difficult to estimate precisely. Reasonableness is judged at formation, not after breach.
Wrong (A): The clause does not require actual damages to exceed the liquidated amount; it is enforceable based on the reasonableness estimate at formation.
Wrong (C): Mere agreement to the clause is insufficient; courts will strike down disproportionately large amounts as unenforceable penalties.
Wrong (D): Liquidated damages clauses exist precisely because exact harm is difficult to prove; requiring proof of the exact amount defeats their purpose.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11194;

-- Q11195: Business Law: Contracts [hard/L4]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Newton''s modification is enforceable under UCC 2-209 in good faith; Archer''s fails for lack of consideration","Both modifications are enforceable because both parties voluntarily agreed to the new terms","Neither modification is enforceable because both lack the required new consideration","Newton''s modification fails because it is oral; Archer''s is enforceable under common law"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under UCC Section 2-209(1), a contract for the sale of goods may be modified without new consideration if made in good faith. Newton''s steel shortage provides a legitimate commercial reason. Under common law, modifications require new consideration, so Archer''s promise to perform the same work for more money violates the pre-existing duty rule.
Wrong (B): Common law requires new consideration for modifications; voluntary agreement alone is insufficient for the services contract.
Wrong (C): The UCC eliminates the consideration requirement for goods modifications made in good faith, so Newton''s modification is valid.
Wrong (D): The UCC Statute of Frauds may apply to the goods contract, but the key distinction is consideration, not form; Archer''s common law modification fails for lack of new consideration.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11195;

-- Q11197: Business Law: Contracts [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A promise made under duress that can be voided by the coerced party","A promise to perform an act that is illegal or against public policy","A promise made without the other party''s knowledge or awareness","A statement appearing to be a promise but imposing no actual obligation"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under Restatement (Second) of Contracts Section 71, consideration requires a bargained-for exchange in which each party incurs a legal detriment. An illusory promise imposes no actual obligation on the promisor and therefore cannot serve as valid consideration, rendering the agreement unenforceable.
Wrong (A): A promise made under duress is voidable, not illusory; it creates an obligation that the coerced party may choose to rescind.
Wrong (B): A promise to perform an illegal act involves illegality of subject matter, which is a separate formation defense from illusory promises.
Wrong (C): Lack of the other party''s knowledge relates to assent or offer issues, not to whether the promise itself imposes an obligation.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11197;
